In article <7auoph$95o$1@freyja.bart.nl>,
M.A.R. Janssen <marj@SPAM.ME.AND.ILL.SEND.VANDAMME.bart.nl> wrote:
>When I try to install Red Hat Linux 5.2 on my AXPCI33 board I get as far as
>the phase when the Kernel should boot, I select the option 'linux' but then
>I get an error message "not enough memory,
>
>My board has 16 Mb memory, but no cache. Is that the problem?

Dunno about 5.2, but the earlier versions installed fine on 16 MB of RAM
(which is all I have in my Noname) *iff* you told MILO about it -- otherwise
MILO would assume that Nonames always have 24 MB.  Dunno if this is fixed now,
and I forget the exact syntax but it's one of the MILO environment variables
which I believe you can display with "printenv", mem_size or MEM_SIZE or
something like that, it should be set to the actual memory size in megabytes.

Of course, it certainly couldn't *hurt* to add more memory, although then
you could get in a silly situation of using only 24 MB instead of whatever
you really have, which is a waste.  So either way, you should deal with this,
assuming that MILO still doesn't know how to autosize on Nonames.

John Wilson
D Bit



