Alex Rhomberg <rhomberg@ife.ee.ethz.ch> wrote:

> I'm a bit confused about the differences between UX and LX boards.

Quant-X have some reasonable information at their web site
<URL:http://www.quant-x.at/>, although they try hard to hide it inside
an abysmal jungle of frames and image maps.

The respective product sheets are:
  <URL:http://www.quant-x.at/Alpha/frm3ATXLX.htm>
  <URL:http://www.quant-x.at/Alpha/frm3ATXUX.htm>

> On http://www.alpha-processor.com the only difference is the onboard
> SCSI and Ethernet on UX, while LX has one more 64 Bit PCI Slot.

The LX has
  2x 64-bit PCI
  2x 32-bit PCI
  2x ISA

The UX has
  1x 64-bit PCI
  5x 32-bit PCI
  on-board 10/100 ethernet
  on-board UW SCSI
  1x ISA

Also, with the UX you have a choice of either 2 or 4MB third level
cache. The Quant-X page mentions a 4MB cache option for the LX board but
I have yet to see an actual offer for this.

IMHO, the UX is clearly the superior board. Without on-board peripherals
four PCI slots don't cut it nowadays. You need video, SCSI, and network.
That leaves one slot to play with sound, TV, etc. And ISA, apart from
being just lame, is pretty dead and will be more so within the next two
years.

Alas, the SRM console is only available for the LX, but not for the UX.
This is less of a concern with Linux, but if you want to give BSD a try
you either need SRM or make the effort to come up with a MILO equivalent
for BSD.

> So how fast is the memory really? L3 Cache and DRAM both 128Bit @ 66MHz?

"Memory Subsystem:
   128-bit data bus
   1.056 GB/sec bandwith"

