A while back I asked what effect a dead battery could have on a Multia.  My
Multia from Starship had been running NT Server for a couple years before
refusing to boot presumably due to a dead battery.

I found a replacement battery at the local techie computer store.  It is a
"Daytona computer battery", part # COMP-11P, equivalent to a #844.  It cost
about $15.

Plugging in his battery had no effect.  I still saw nothing but a square
cursor in the bottom left of the screen.  So I bought another Multia from
Genesis Computer on the net and tried its motherboard in my existing case.
Zip, nada, same thing.

Turns out the INTERNAL 2.5" IDE DRIVE I use for a swap file drive kept the
box from booting.  After diconnecting the drive the box booted and l was
able to reconnect the drive later.

The Genesis Multia came with a 5.25" SCSI drive mounting bracket.  I think
Starship said that's a bit much for a Multia which has minimal power and
bigtime heat.  Also, the Starship box came with an external SCSI connector
which the Genesis box lacks.  Bummer.

In general, both Multias work fine and both suppliers are great.  Once I get
some RAM for the new box I'll run Linux on it.  I promise.

Anyway, bottom line is yank any weird stuff in the box when you replace the
battery.  Otherwise you may be left scratching your head or buying an
unnecessary replacement.

Thanks for all past and future replies.

--- Greg A





