> I've heard rumours that the 164LX SRM firmware works on the Samsung UX
> boards, because they're basically LX clones with on-board ethernet and
> SCSI added.
> 
> Has anybody actually tried it and knows first hand that it works?
> Especially that "show config" correctly reports the presence of the
> ethernet and SCSI controllers.  The 164LX SRM console supports the
> 21143 and the Symbios/NCR 875, so it should work if the firmware works
> at all.
> 
> I'm aware of the licensing issues though.  I'll try to resolve these
> separately.  BTW, does anybody know whether a purchase of the EBSDK
> would entitle me to use the SRM console on a UX board?  I'll probably
> have to ask TCFKAD/Compaq about this.
> 
> I consider purchasing an LX or UX2 board and won't buy a UX if I can't
> run SRM on it.  If I can, the UX2 would be better, because it's cheaper
> than an LX + SCSI + ethernet and also more expandable AFAIK.
> 
> joerch

Nope, the SRM cannot work on the UX motherboards, simply because there
are simple differences.  For example, the SROM that loads into the
internal cache architecture is different.  The UX flash rom contains
SROM code AND ARCs BIOs.  

It's not out of the question, but there are slight subtle differences in
registers, etc. . .

If Deskstation/Samsung developed the board to support the same BIOs as
the LX motherboard, then you would have a killer motherboard.

