Alex Rhomberg <rhomberg@ife.ee.ethz.ch> writes:
> I'm a bit confused about the differences between UX and LX boards.
> On http://www.alpha-processor.com the only difference is the onboard
> SCSI and Ethernet on UX, while LX has one more 64 Bit PCI Slot.

There are other differences.  For instance the LX has 4 slots for
SDRAM whereas the UX has 6, IIRC.


> Both have a 128 Bit datapath and 66 MHz system clock, which is a bit
> slow?

Look at the STREAM benchmarks.  These boards get about 250 MB/s which
is OK.  A top-of-the-line PC can beat them by a bit, at 300-ish.
(BTW, the new DP264 Alpha boards get well over 1000 - yummy!)

Also, the Alphas typically have 2 or 4 MB cache, so most processes
don't care so much about the main RAM.


> Compare that to the specs on harddata, where they write that memory on
> the LX is 100 MHz (more than specs) [...]

The busses on Miata/LX/UX are 66 or 62.5 MHz but 100 MHz SDRAM is
recommended.  At first 83 MHz SDRAM was, but then PC100 came along and
the price of 100 MHz SDRAM became reasonable so one might as well take
the extra safety margin.


> Did anybody compare a UX PCI 32 Bit PCI slot (which is on a secondary
> PCI Bus via PCI-PCI Bridge) with the performance of an LX 32 Bit PCI
> slot?

Not me.


Bye,
  Rob.



