: > Now, the $3 question is why would you like Linux on a machine
: > which is licensed to run DU...

: Now, the $3 question is why you're posting flamebait like this to a
: Linux newsgroup?

It was certainly *not* a flamebait - even though some of the people
here with rather more adrenaline in their veins than is good for their
health did take it for one.

I am honestly interested in why people would like to run Linux on
a box used primarily for computational work (as was implied in the
Fortran request which started this thread) and licensed to run
OSF/DU/Tru64/whatever. The VM argument certainly does not count 
- a DU box properly tuned for a memory-intensive job *will* 
need a few megabytes more for the kernel than a Linux system
(mostly because it is not possible to tune the minimum size
of the buffer to less than 1% of the system memory), but not 
more than a few. IMO, a convenience of using a professional-
quality Fortran compiler, robust NIS support and reasonably
fast NFS more than compensates for that.

I do run Linux on my desktop alpha (because a. it is not licensed
to run DU; b. I hate CDU; and c. our CD recorder crashes DU hard),
but I wouldn't dream of running the version I use (RH 5.2) on our
production machines... for various reasons I am not going to discuss
here lest I am going to be accused of flamebaiting.

Regards,

/Serge.P



