Much the same experience as I had with a Millenium.

I also had similar problems with a couple of S3 Trio 64 cards.  With one I
did manage to get into X but it was horribly unstable so I left it for a while.

>So, if you're successfully using a PCI display adapter in a Multia please
>get in touch; I would like to hear about the software and boot method you
>are using. 

Recently I got an S3 Virge DX 4MB card, the intention was to swap it for a
Trio 64 from a machine at work which would fit into the Multia.  As it turned
out the Virge was about 5mm too long to fit in the Multia if I played by
the book.  I didn't, I slotted it between the HD and floppy and it works ;-)

I generally run it in 16bit mode at 1024x768 which is nice and has good
acceleration with SVGA driver.  I don't use the S3V driver because
it wasn't as quick.  I can run it in 32 bit but it is a bit slower and
I don't really need more than 64K colours.  Its a joy after the TGA 8bit.

Since I upgraded to XFree86 3.3.3 the machine has been very solid.  I'm
messing about with kernel 2.2.1 at the moment so any instabilities could
be down to that.

I am shortly going to put an IDE 3.2 gig drive in the machine (managed
to get a 44pin IDE cable for it) and that will help the fitment of this
slightly oversized card because the IDE interface is on the motherboard
whereas the SCSI is on the PCI riser card.  Its all a bit tight in there!

I boot with MILO from ARC, works fine.  Compile a fresh kernel with TGA
support turned off and VGA support on and it will all work nicely.  The
thing with these PCI video cards seems to be trying several until you hit
one that works unfortunately.

