In article <36C7F444.8CFDDEE7@teor.fis.uc.pt>, Fernando Nogueira wrote:
>Hi.
>
>We have a "parallel" machine built up of 20 DEC Personal Workstations
>500 au, running
>Digital Unix and are thinking of moving them to Linux. The only reason
>that made us
>buy 20 licenses for DU was the Fortran compiler. Is there any way of
>using it with
>Linux? We have the DU liceneses and also a DEC Campus license.

To the best of my knowledge, you cannot run the DUNIX Fortran compiler
under Linux.  At least, I've never heard of people doing so.  On the
other hand, I don't see any immediate reasons why it wouldn't work, so
you might want to try and see what happens.

Even if it turns out that you cannot run the compiler under Linux, you
will almost certainly be able to run the compiled programs under
Linux.  So, you could dedicate one machine to run DUNIX and the
compiler, while running the binaries via DUNIX emulation under Linux
on the other 19 machines.

-andy



