On 20 Feb 1999 03:35:47 GMT, Shana Tackett <sltackett@worldnet.att.net> wrote:
>Check the file /etc/sysconfig/clock. It should have the following lines in
>it:
>
>UTC=false
>ARC=true

This will fix the date after install but all the files put on disc during
install will have the wrong date.  The solution is to copy the date binary
to a floppy and when you do the install you can mount the floppy before you
actually start installing files (do this before you mount the CD) and run
date to set the correct date and time.  Then carry on with the install as
normal, on my UDB this work fine and the dates of all my files reflect
when I did the install rather than giving dates of 2018.

RedHat really should add an option to do this to their install process
because it is something which annoys the hell out of a lot of people.

