Hi,

In comp.sys.dec santaromita@my-dejanews.com wrote:

> well, this is the all point: i don't know if, for the sort of application i
> am interested in, (which is fortran programming and running of codes for the
> approximation of solution of differential equations) i should get more
> INTEGER or FP performance. my only guess is that these codes will run tons of
> loops for infinitely many times; i have no idea if this will be more an
> integer or fp duty.

If it will run lots of loops, few minutes per loop more or less will have
a great influence.

> $7k is way out of the range; i was more like thinking about:
> alpha 21164- 600 with 4m of cache
> dual pentium- 400
> G3 300-333(???)

To really find out, you have to run tests with an example of the kind of
soft you want to use. Prepare, if possible, a small test program in Fortran
which does that kind of things you are after and contact people with these
systems for a test run with a lot of loops. That's the best way to find out.
As I have seen from other threads, you never can really tell from benchmarks
or other standardised tests whether they are true for your specific
application. And make sure, that the compilation is done with the system
specific optimisations. Non-optimised and optimised compilations may be
worlds apart!



Regards,

Kerstin



