the cited author.]

Check the debian-alpha mail list archives and share your experience 
there. There was quite a bit of discussion last December. 

http://www.debian.org/Lists-Archives/debian-alpha-9812/threads.html

You will also find a previous discussion on installing RedHat to a Jensen 
in this news group, titled 'RH 5.2 on a Jensen?' starting on 11/14/98.

To make your early efforts easier, dedicate a drive to aboot. This will 
allow you to get through the RedHat fdisk/diskdruid section targeting the 
second drive without blowing away your aboot environment. If you don't do 
this you will have to play a risky game of trying to maintain both the 
standard fdisk partition table and the BSD partition table, which is done 
with paper and pencil as you repeatedly fix one or both partition tables 
to reflect the location of data you've already placed on the disk. I did 
this to get through my initial RedHat installation. It wasn't fun.

While I am successfully maintaining a single partition on my aboot drive, 
I have been unsuccessful at maintaining multiple partitions. My problem 
being that whenever I modify aboot or the kernel that coexists with it 
all but my primary partition are blown away. I'm sure I've missed the 
significance of some detail that is causing this problem. I have my aboot 
drive partitioned with a large (10Mb?), unused space at the beginning to 
hold aboot and a bootable kernel.

If you figure out methods of maintaining aboot and multiple active 
partitions, that allows the RedHat installation to recognize the drive's 
partitions and lets you fiddle with the aboot data space, let me know in 
great detail, because I have not succeeded at this.

Good luck.
- Peter Mott


In article <Al2u2.1355$ZE4.5339@news010.image.dk>, tsPorsgaard@image.dk 
says...
> I have a big problem when trying to install RedHat 5.1 on my Jensen: RH 5.1
