: (switchable MS/PC) on serial /dev/cua0 (dos com1). I've also noticed
: that as linux boots it finds a PS/2 mouse and installs the driver, but
: strangely enough if I remove all trace of the mouse and serial
: connections from the MLB, it still finds it after rebooting the system??
: I've tried the Lynx on my Intel NT system and it works and my trackball
: and another old mouse on the Alpha without success. I've checked with
: the Alpha manual for any mention of a jumper for PS/2 ports but there
: isn't anything, the board isn't even fitted with PS/2 ports, only the AT
There are two versions of the AXPci33, one with just AT-kbd  and another
with PS/2 mouse and PS/2-kbd.
: keyboard connector and therefore a serial mouse. In the console there is
: no facility for configuring ports of any sort so I find it hard to
: believe that it is hardware problem. This is backed up by the fact that
: Milo's command prompt goes haywire if the mouse is plugged in.
:     I've already read through all the FAQ's and threads at the Red Hat
: site as well as a number of different news groups but to no avail. Maybe
: I've missed something quite simple but I would be grateful for any light
: that anybody can throw on this.
a) I think the ARC takes input from and directs output to COM1 (cua0). I
don't know whether this behaviour can be changed. (My manual only states
that WinNT setup will go haywire if a PS/2-mouse is attached. No problems
with setup, but NT's unstable, anyway. But Linux works like a charm.)
Try connecting the mouse to COM2 (cua1)

b) Check the wiring ! According to Table 17 of the manual it is as follows:
Pin	1 : DCD
	2 : Serial Out (=TXD)
	3 : GND
	4 : RTS
	5 : RI (Ring Indicator)
	6 : Serial In (=RXD)
	7 : DTR
	8 : DSR
	9 : CTS
                 10: Not Connected

The Serial Ports are RS423 compliant.

Good luck !
Pete

