>>>>> "RC" == Rob Clark <rclark@dotwireless.com> writes:

RC> I finally have my UDB installed (w/ debian) and running, but I
RC> have one last problem.  I need to be able to make my UDB boot
RC> un-attended, without a monitor.  Does anyone have any info on how
RC> to do this?
RC> Right now I have milo on a floppy disk, so I need to
RC> type "boot dva0" from the SRM prompt, and then "boot
RC> hda2:/vmlinux.gz root=/dev/hda2" from milo.  Is it possible to
RC> automate these two steps?

You have basicly two choices here:
use ARC and read that HOWTO
use SRM to load MILO from your disk

The following procedure is still kinda rough, so it isn't up on my
pages anywhere, but:
---begin quote-----

   and I realize that I need a BSD disklabel on the disk to use
   swriteboot and install a boot-block on the disk.

   This being my first install, I use sdisklabel and set BSD
   partitions in not quite the right places. This of course hoses my
   installation :(

   On my next install I try to use fdisk right at the start to put a
   BSD label on the disk; the installer doesn't let me, complaining
   about corrupt label; eventually I give up on that idea and put a
   DOS style partion table on the disk. After about an hour I get sick
   of it hanging on me (the installer had just stopped for fifteen
   minutes on the same package and same place) and reset.

   The next run I was smarter and left the room so I wouldn't be there
   to watch it hang for about 20 minutes at a time in four different
   spots, although I did check up on it occasionally.


   Not anxious to reinstall RedHat again I look for a copy of
   Minlabel, finding that while it isn't installed by default it is
   part of the distribution, use it to carefully, albeit with a bit of
   anxiety, relabel my disk and reboot. I then try swriteboot to put
   milo in as my bootloader. No good, linux still thinks I've got a
   DOS partition table. I then try fdisk, which, when I tell it to use
   BSD labels, finds the label I wrote with minlabel. I write it out
   again and swriteboot works this time.


   Now that I have a boot loader and can be fairly sure I won't need
   to do another net install I compile a bunch of stuff that I'm
   pretty sure I'll need or use, then reboot again, this time looking
   for variables/flags to set so that I can get the system to boot
   automagically.

   Reading through the docs I find the environment variables for milo,
   and set them so that milo will boot linux once it has loaded:

   setenv BOOT_DEV sda2

   setenv BOOT_FILE vmlinux.gz


   setenv MEMORY_SIZE 64


   (I got my UDB from cpumicromart along with two 32MB simms, making
   24MB the wrong size)


   setenv BOOT_STRING root=/dev/sda2

   setenv AUTOBOOT_TIMEOUT 24

   That done I set about the process of getting SRM to boot
   milo. First I set bootdef_dev to dka0, which means that I can now
   just type `boot' and have SRM load milo which loads linux.

(trimmed stuff)

  the (firmware update) readme had some interesting data in it as well:

   >>>set pwrup_test off
   >>>

   keeps her from doing the TGA selftest at boot-time ^_^

   and not only is there a >>>PWRUP_UPDATE command, there is a
   pwrup_update SRM environment variable.


   >>>set pwrup_update off
   >>>

   has fixed the `no disk in dva0' errors, but I still haven't gotten
   her to boot automaticly. I'll keep trying, however.


   Today someone responded with a usefull answer to my post on usenet:


   >>>set auto_action boot

  makes her boot automagically, unattended.
---end quote---

Using ARC may be faster and safer; however, I personally don't want to
use something with a M$ copyright to boot linux ^_^

