Carlos Vidal (carlos@tarkus.se) wrote:
: We work with nuclear reactor calculations, and for this kind of number crunching
: a PII-400MHz runs half as fast as the fastest Alpha, with the difference that the
: PII system costs 30.000 FRF and the Alpha costs 1.6 Million francs. We measure
: this by counting how many standard cases per hour we can run.

Somehow, I think you are comparing apples and oranges. If the FRF/US$ exchange
rate is around 6 (as I think it is), the PII system you mention costs some US$5k,
which is a bit high but not quite outrageous. In this case, the Alpha you are 
talking about is US$250k, which (using list prices) roughly corresponds to 
a 4-cpu AlphaServer 4100.

Given that your problem is small enough to run on a beefed-up PC, this is exactly
the *worst* kind of choice you can make, since this system is simply designed 
(and priced) for a different kind of market. If you are interested in price/performance
ratio, the system you probably want to look at is one of the Digital Personal 
Workstations. The base configuration of DPW 500au (including Unix licence) will
cost you about FF40k (list), with per-cpu performance comparable to that of AS4100.
If you are prepared to run NT or Linux on that system, the price will probably 
go down to somewhere around FF30k list - just the same as your PII system. Of course,
if you are going to spend FF1.6M on hardware, you should be able to negotiate a
much better price. I would estimate that FF1.6M should be able to buy you at least
50 headless DPW 500au's, and probably more than that. If you want to get a really
small number of systems, you may also want to go with third-party suppliers, like
Aspen Systems - they have lower list prices, but do not go down as steeply as 
Digital/Compaq for larger purchases.

Same money will likely buy you roughly the same number of decent PII systems
(it is much harder to negoriate a good discount for a relatively small number
of systems in the PC market), at twice the price/performance of the DPW 500au
solution. Not bad for an "overpriced" workstation, in my opinion.

: Depending on how many users will be calculating at the same time I would
: suggest you to buy a cluster of PII or dual PII and run them with beowulf.

Unless your applications run unusually fast on PII (and unusually slow on
Alphas, in which case you should really take a close look at your code,
since something is probably wrong with it) I would recommend against this. 
It is just as easy to build a cluster of, say, DPWs (or DS20-s, if you 
really want the best per-cpu performance your money can buy you at the moment) 
as from generic PII boxes, and you get much better price/performance ratio.

You should also keep in mind that once the size of the cluster increases
beyound a handful of nodes, the progressively larger fraction of the total 
cost goes towards inter-node communication hardware, support infrastructure
(e.g. cooling and power) and maintenance costs (both hardware and software).
In this respect, it is usually more cost-efficient to keep the size of your
cluster to the absolute minimum adequate for solving your problem. Better
single-CPU performance certainly helps here.

Regards,

/Serge.P



